From: Nex package maintainers
Subject: global pkcs11.conf and system .module directories walk the three UAPI tiers
Source: Nex downstream patch for p11-kit 0.25.5
Upstream: not submitted; needs agreement on a machine-generated transient tier

p11-kit compiles a single global settings file, P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE
("/etc/pkcs11/pkcs11.conf" on this distribution), and two system module
directories, P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_MODULES ("/etc/pkcs11/modules") and
P11_PACKAGE_CONFIG_MODULES ("/usr/share/p11-kit/modules"), with no
transient "/run" tier at all. load_config_from_file() also only ever
filled in a same-named module file's unset fields from whichever file
loaded first, so a later, higher-priority file could never fully replace
an earlier one; it could only patch in fields the earlier file omitted.

This patch adds a "/run/pkcs11" transient tier to both families and
changes the module-directory combination so that the vendor
(P11_PACKAGE_CONFIG_MODULES), runtime ("/run/pkcs11/modules"), and
administrator (P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_MODULES) directories are first combined
among themselves, with a higher tier's same-named file completely
replacing (not merging into) a lower tier's file of the same name. That
combined system result then only fills in config names the user
directory left unset, exactly as a single system directory always did,
so user-configuration semantics (user-config: none/merge/only) are
unchanged.

conf.c's _p11_conf_load_modules() gains a run_dir parameter between
package_dir and system_dir. All four callers in test-conf.c pass a
nonexistent fixture path for it, leaving every existing fixture-based
regression test unaffected: none of the existing package-modules and
system-modules fixtures share a basename, so the merge-vs-shadow change
between those two tiers has no observable effect on them either.

The global settings file gains the same three-tier walk:
p11_conf_select_system_conf_file() selects the first regular file among
"/etc/pkcs11/pkcs11.conf", "/run/pkcs11/pkcs11.conf", and
"/usr/share/p11-kit/pkcs11.conf". A present higher-tier file masks a
lower one even if empty, because the walk stops at the first regular
file, not the first file with content. When no tier has a regular file,
the reader falls back to the compiled default, which already names the
administrator tier's own path, reproducing the original no-file
behavior exactly.

Every existing override keeps winning outright over the new walk. A
test that calls p11_kit_override_system_files() with a non-NULL
system_conf, or a caller that sets P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE, marks the
selection overridden; the walk then returns NULL and the caller uses
the explicit path exactly as before, without consulting the three
tiers at all. P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_MODULES and P11_PACKAGE_CONFIG_MODULES
keep overriding their respective directories outright, the same as
upstream, because the new run-tier directory is combined additively
alongside them rather than replacing either.

The global variables that back this resolution (p11_config_system_file
and friends, the new p11_config_run_modules, and the tier-selection and
override-tracking logic) move to conf.c, which is linked both into
libp11-kit and, as a separate static copy, directly into the
statically-linked p11-kit command-line tool. modules.c and
print-config.c previously computed the module registry and "p11-kit
print-config" output through two different code paths: the real module
registry (used by C_Initialize and "p11-kit list-modules") went through
modules.c's hidden-visibility globals, while print-config.c read the
compiled P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE and friends directly and never even
called the environment-override hook. Sharing the resolution through
conf.c keeps both readers reporting the exact same paths and lets
print-config.c honor P11_* environment overrides for the first time.

--- a/p11-kit/conf.c
+++ b/p11-kit/conf.c
@@ -72,0 +73,87 @@
+/*
+ * These are global variables to be overridden in tests. They live here,
+ * rather than in modules.c, because conf.c is linked into both libp11-kit
+ * and the statically-linked p11-kit tool, and both readers (the module
+ * registry and "p11-kit print-config") must see the same paths.
+ */
+const char *p11_config_system_file = P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE;
+const char *p11_config_user_file = P11_USER_CONFIG_FILE;
+const char *p11_config_package_modules = P11_PACKAGE_CONFIG_MODULES;
+const char *p11_config_run_modules = "/run/pkcs11/modules";
+const char *p11_config_system_modules = P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_MODULES;
+const char *p11_config_user_modules = P11_USER_CONFIG_MODULES;
+
+/*
+ * Whether p11_config_system_file no longer names the compiled default,
+ * because a test called p11_kit_override_system_files() or a caller set
+ * P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE. Either explicit source is an exact path and
+ * must win outright over the vendor/runtime/administrator tier walk.
+ */
+static bool p11_config_system_file_overridden = false;
+
+void
+p11_conf_mark_system_file_overridden (void)
+{
+	p11_config_system_file_overridden = true;
+}
+
+#ifdef P11_ENV_OVERRIDE_PATHS
+/**
+ * p11_conf_get_paths_from_env:
+ *
+ * Adjusts the global settings file and module directory paths, so that
+ * they can be changed at run-time, based on system environment variables.
+ */
+void
+p11_conf_get_paths_from_env (void)
+{
+	char *path;
+
+	if ((path = secure_getenv ("P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_FILE"))) {
+		p11_config_system_file = path;
+		p11_config_system_file_overridden = true;
+	}
+	if ((path = secure_getenv ("P11_USER_CONFIG_FILE")))
+		p11_config_user_file = path;
+	if ((path = secure_getenv ("P11_PACKAGE_CONFIG_MODULES")))
+		p11_config_package_modules = path;
+	if ((path = secure_getenv ("P11_SYSTEM_CONFIG_MODULES")))
+		p11_config_system_modules = path;
+	if ((path = secure_getenv ("P11_USER_CONFIG_MODULES")))
+		p11_config_user_modules = path;
+}
+#endif
+
+/**
+ * p11_conf_select_system_conf_file:
+ *
+ * Selects the global pkcs11.conf that a caller did not name explicitly:
+ * the first regular file among the administrator, runtime, and
+ * package-owned tiers. Returns a newly allocated path, or %NULL when
+ * either an explicit override is in effect (p11_config_system_file
+ * already names the exact file to use) or none of the three tiers has
+ * a regular file (the caller then keeps using the compiled default,
+ * which is the administrator tier's own path).
+ */
+char *
+p11_conf_select_system_conf_file (void)
+{
+	static const char *const tiers[] = {
+		"/etc/pkcs11/pkcs11.conf",
+		"/run/pkcs11/pkcs11.conf",
+		"/usr/share/p11-kit/pkcs11.conf",
+	};
+	struct stat sb;
+	unsigned i;
+
+	if (p11_config_system_file_overridden)
+		return NULL;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < sizeof (tiers) / sizeof (tiers[0]); i++) {
+		if (stat (tiers[i], &sb) == 0 && S_ISREG (sb.st_mode))
+			return strdup (tiers[i]);
+	}
+
+	return NULL;
+}
+
